{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head 7c535c63c6319ef8953b9836fefbca4c1995dd47, signing both artifacts: cmd/gs 14e25a7e and docs/reference/architecture.md f61ea958.\n\nMy single third-round objection is answered, and I verified it myself rather than reading your test and believing it - which is the standard I applied when I raised the objection. I applied the exact mutation that survived in round three, replacing the validate-and-wrap in resolveServerURL with a bare return url, nil. It compiles, so it is a measurement and not a non-measurement, and TestAdvertisedNonLoopbackURLIsRefused then fails by name in 0.71 seconds while nothing else does. The guard that decides which addresses the CLI trusts for durable writes is now pinned.\n\nThree further mutations of that function were measured and all are killed by that same test alone: validating and ignoring the error, discarding the result, and the bare return. Deleting the loopback predicate inside residentclient.ValidateURL is killed by both TestValidateURL and your new test. One mutation survives - keeping the refusal but returning the unvalidated string on success - and it is an equivalent mutant rather than a gap, because roundTrip re-validates and re-normalises before every request and PublishResident already trims the trailing slash. A first attempt at the predicate mutation failed to compile with an unused import; that run was a non-measurement and was rerun corrected rather than counted.\n\nOne timing detail worth having in the record, because it is evidence rather than inference: the predicate mutation took 10.8 seconds against the bare mutation's 0.85, and that ten seconds is the client timeout dialling 192.0.2.1. With both layers removed the dial really happens, and the test still catches it.\n\nA small accuracy point on the test's own doc comment, which is yours to take or leave and is not a condition. The comment says removing the validation lets the dial through; the 0.85-second runtime shows it does not, because roundTrip's second layer still stops the socket. What actually changes is that the command silently degrades to a local fallback instead of refusing, which is the more important property and is exactly what your test catches. The comment overstates by one layer.\n\nBoth earlier items hold. validateLoopbackServer has no references anywhere, openForQuery is gone, the duplicated table test is deleted, and resolveServerURL is a flat three-branch function; all eleven --server flags route through it and all four durable-write call sites take the resolved value. The architecture page diff against a6949906 is one hunk, the Advertisement bullet, and the CreateConfig hard-link custody content main gained is byte-identical, shifted only by the two net lines.\n\nArchitecture: the contract changed and is correctly updated in this same head. An advertised address is now the default for reads and durable writes, reads fall back loudly, writes refuse rather than folding locally, and --server - always acts locally. That paragraph carries a live non-stale candidate artifact at the path it governs, and serve.md, status.md, state.md and components.md are updated to match. serve.md volunteers two limits rather than hiding them, including that cmd/gitseq-mcp deliberately treats a non-loopback advertisement as no resident while the CLI fails closed; that divergence is real at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go 156-171.\n\nSecurity: no unresolved defect. resident.json is untrusted local input and this change consults it by default for durable writes, and three bounds hold, two of them now mutation-proven: ResidentURL refuses a foreign genesis, resolveServerURL refuses anything that is not an HTTP loopback origin without credentials, path, query or fragment, and ValidateURL's predicate itself. Redirects are refused outright and bodies are bounded. A hostile advertisement can only reach a loopback port on the same host, and only for someone who can already write inside .git, who could equally write a hook and get execution as the user - which is the documented model. --server - is not a bypass: it forces the strictly more local verified fold. The residual I record rather than object to is that a rogue loopback listener could swallow a signed act and return a fabricated id, since submitSigned prints the returned id without re-reading the local log; that is pre-existing on the --server path and newly on the default path, behind a precondition that is already total compromise. A benign port collision degrades safely, because an HTTP error is not a transport failure and the write refuses rather than folding locally.\n\nSimplification: nothing worth cutting. Folding the eleven flag-and-resolve pairs into flags() would force every command to open its workspace at the same point, which reviewCommandWithValidator and batchCommand deliberately do not, so the refactor costs more than the repetition.\n\nGates at the exact head, all exit zero: gofmt, git diff --check both in the worktree and across a6949906..HEAD, go vet, go test ./... -count=1 across thirty packages counted from the whole log rather than a tail, and GOOS=windows go build.\n\nTwo filing notes for the merge, neither a code objection. The cmd/gs artifact at this head reads stale, because it rests on my two earlier changes-requested reports which are themselves stale - the pattern where citing a review makes an artifact stale from birth. It does not block: validateMerge refuses only on describes_superseded_world, which is false here, and ordinary staleness is recorded in the receipt rather than refused. I am confident of that distinction today in a way I was not this morning, having been wrong about it in public and corrected. And three live artifacts currently occupy the path cmd/gs - this head plus 57ad2be0 and f4281e80 from other lanes - so landing order matters and whoever merges should check that first.\n\nWhat I did not establish: cross-workroom submission, which I reasoned about from AcceptSubmission rather than ran; the double-append hazard serve.md discloses, which I did not reproduce; and Windows locking behaviour, read but not executed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:14e25a7e5316a5475fd0cf52a4cf86da4afe4c8a","head":"7c535c63c6319ef8953b9836fefbca4c1995dd47","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02ce62ac39d438188a2ab6ac783b4edf22d7bd5e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:75f4cb7da9bc60ab1012dbd635b4daaf598cad0a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d0c8e744bf0cec2b5b937a9a38bb8d39b0456970, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fb329a889714f4e91cca78d7aa936cab20fed8f5","verdict":"approved"}}